The failure is thrown by Vercel’s Next.js build-adapter in its very last step, when it tries to package everything that Next.js put in “the build directory”.
It simply can’t find the file it always expects to be there:

<project-root>/app/.next/routes-manifest.json
So either
• the file was never generated, or
• it was generated somewhere else, or
• it was generated and later deleted/moved.

The only things that influence that are (a) your next.config.js, (b) the build/post-build scripts, or (c) the project “Root Directory” setting in the Vercel dashboard.

Below is the shortest path I know to pinpoint which of those three is responsible and how to fix it.

Reproduce the exact Vercel build locally
1.1 From the project root run the identical command Vercel runs:

  npx vercel build --debug

 (The “--debug” flag shows every file copy Vercel performs.)
1.2 When the command finishes, inspect what was produced:

  tree -L 2 .vercel/output
  tree -L 2 .next
  tree -L 2 app/.next         # the directory Vercel complained about
1.3 If “routes-manifest.json” really is missing everywhere, the issue is in the Next.js build phase (steps 2–3 below).
If the file exists but in a different path, the issue is a misplaced distDir or rootDirectory setting (step 4).
If the file exists in the right path locally but Vercel still fails in the cloud, something deletes it during post-build (step 5).

Make sure Next.js is actually generating the manifest
2.1 Run a plain Next.js build:

  rm -rf .next
  npm run build      # this runs: prisma generate && next build
2.2 Look for the manifest:

  ls .next/routes-manifest.json

 • If present → Next.js is fine – go to step 3.  
 • If absent   → Next.js never created it.  Nine times out of ten that
   happens because “output: 'export'” is set.  That mode does a static
   export and intentionally skips server files such as the manifest.

   Fix: remove “output: 'export'” (or change it to 'standalone').
Confirm no one deletes the manifest after the build
3.1 Grep your repo for anything that touches “.next” after the build:

  grep -R "\.next" --exclude-dir=node_modules .
3.2 Pay special attention to your “postbuild” script.
In your logs the only command executed is

   "postbuild": "prisma generate"

 Prisma won’t touch .next, so if that’s all you have you can rule out
 deletion.  If you see any “rimraf”, “rm -rf .next”, or “next export”
 here – that is the culprit.
Check for a distDir or rootDirectory mismatch
4.1 In next.config.js:

  module.exports = {
    distDir: 'app/.next',   // ← THIS is legal but often mis-set
  }

 • If you have a custom distDir, verify it’s exactly “app/.next”.
   A missing or extra segment (e.g. 'app/next' or 'build/.next')
   will make Vercel look in the wrong folder.

 • Temporarily comment the distDir out and rebuild locally:

     // distDir: 'app/.next',

   If vercel build now passes, the path is wrong – fix or remove it.
4.2 In the Vercel dashboard open
Project → Settings → General → Root Directory.

 • If it is set to “app”, Vercel starts the build in /app, not in the
   repository root.  Leave it on “app” OR remove the custom distDir, but
   don’t do both.  Having both makes the manifest end up in
   “/app/app/.next” while Vercel looks in “/app/.next”.
Double-check Prisma generate is not running in the wrong folder
Prisma uses the working directory it is invoked from. If someone changed “postbuild” to cd into another directory before running prisma generate, the shell might land inside “app/” and then back out, accidentally moving files. Make sure the script is exactly:

"postbuild": "prisma generate"
(no cd, no && rm -rf …).

Once fixed, verify in CI
Commit the change, push, and let Vercel rebuild.
The build log should now show:

Uploading build outputs...
Build completed. Deployed to https://<your-url>.vercel.app
Quick reference checklist
☐ next.config.js does NOT set output:'export' (or it is intentional).
☐ distDir is either undefined (default “.next”) or correct and unique.
☐ Vercel “Root Directory” matches (repo root vs. /app).
☐ No script (build, postbuild, prisma, custom) removes or moves .next.
☐ After npm run build, .next/routes-manifest.json exists locally.
☐ vercel build --debug also finds the manifest in the same place.

Follow the list top-to-bottom; in >90 % of cases the problem is fixed by: • removing output:'export', OR
• deleting an incorrect distDir entry, OR
• aligning Vercel’s Root Directory with your repo structure.

That’s all you need to get the build passing again. Good luck!